Yes. Your additions make the why much clearer, especially the personal observation that engineers can spend years discovering capabilities that already exist elsewhere in the organization. That should stay. I’d compress the whole thing to these five paragraphs:

Summary

Enterprise Technology Census with Agentic Classification & Workforce Intelligence — An automated system that uses our existing enterprise URL inventory to create a searchable index of internal applications and tools, identify the technologies behind them, and provide visibility into technology adoption and workforce needs. It combines a low-cost deterministic Python classifier with targeted agentic analysis for endpoints that are ambiguous or require deeper investigation.

Description

As an engineer at PNC, it has taken me years to discover many of the tools, integrations, and capabilities other teams have already built. Having a central, searchable way to discover what exists could have accelerated much of my own work—and the same is likely true for engineers across the organization. Beyond discovery, we also have an opportunity to use this information to understand what technologies we are actually running, where those technologies are concentrated, how adoption is changing, and what that may imply for modernization and future workforce needs.

I already have a working demo that builds on an existing inventory of URLs mapped to application mnemonics and ownership. The solution consolidates related paths under their primary hosts, creates an alphabetized searchable index of applications and tools, and uses lightweight HTTP inspection followed by deeper browser-based inspection when necessary to classify endpoints—for example, UI versus API—and identify observable technologies. Each indexed entry can also be connected to its application/business description and ownership information, turning a URL inventory into something engineers can actually navigate and use.

The next step is a cost-effective combination of deterministic automation and agentic reasoning. Known patterns are handled quickly by the Python classifier. URLs that remain low-confidence, unfamiliar, or require deeper analysis are placed into an exception queue for an agent to investigate during scheduled nightly or weekly processing. Rather than paying for AI analysis of every endpoint, agents are used only where they add value. More importantly, when an agent discovers a repeatable pattern, that discovery can be reviewed and converted into a Jira enhancement for the deterministic classifier. This creates a continuous improvement loop: classifier → exception → agent discovery → reviewed reusable pattern → classifier upgrade, reducing agent usage and cost while increasing the capability and certainty of the system over time.

Finally, associating detected technologies with application mnemonics, teams, and organizational ownership creates the foundation for an enterprise technology census. We could measure how many applications and teams use technologies such as Java, JavaScript, Go, or Python; visualize their distribution across the organization; track adoption and modernization trends; and develop team-level estimates of where technical skills are concentrated. It would not claim exact per-person proficiency, but it could provide a much stronger evidence base for technology strategy, training, recruiting, workforce planning, and platform investment. The long-term goal is to answer two deceptively simple questions: “What capabilities and tools do we already have?” and “What are we actually running, where, and what does that tell us about where we should invest next?”

I like this substantially better than the earlier version. Paragraph 1 is the pitch, 2 is why anybody should care, 3 proves you’ve already built something, 4 contains the genuinely interesting agentic architecture, and 5 elevates it into enterprise strategy.

Also, your wording about the workforce estimate is important: team/application-based estimation, not individual skill measurement. Keep that distinction. It makes the proposal more credible and avoids claiming the data can tell leadership something it actually can’t.